===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|stryngen}}, {{m|enm|strengen}}, from the noun (see above).

====Verb====
{{en-verb|||strung|past2=strang|past2_qual=obsolete or nonstandard|strung}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To put (items) on a string.
#: {{ux|en|You can '''string''' these beads on to this cord to make a colorful necklace.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To put strings on (something).
#: {{ux|en|It is difficult to '''string''' a tennis racket properly.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To form into a string or strings, as a substance which is stretched, or people who are moving along, etc.
# {{lb|en|intransitive|billiards}} To drive the ball against the end of the table and back, in order to determine which player is to open the game.
# {{lb|en|birdwatching}} To deliberately state that a certain [[bird]] is present when it is not; to knowingly [[mislead]] other [[birder]]s about the occurrence of a bird, especially a [[rarity]]; to [[misidentify]] a common bird as a rare [[species]].
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1980|author=Bill Oddie|title=Bill Oddie's Little Black Bird Book|page=81|passage=To be honest, you'd be better off trying to '''string''' a Skylark as a Richard's Pipit rather than as a Pectoral Sandpiper.}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=2005|author=w:Sean Dooley|title=The Big Twitch|publisher=Allen and Unwin|location=Sydney|page=67|passage=For instance he might see a White-eared Honeyeater, a not uncommon bird in the heathy areas at Bunyip, but in his excitement to call it, something in his brain scrambled and came out as: `White-cheeked Honeyeater!' White-cheeked Honeyeater is an absolute stonking crippler in Victoria, but Stu was not actually trying to '''string''' a rarity, he'd just got such a flood of new information swirling around his brain that sometimes it got jumbled up.}}

